{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And when he sells</b> — the verse clothed the matter in the seller and not in the buyer, to say that this one has no possibility of being acquired except from her father, for she does not sell herself, nor does a court sell her (Mekhilta). And it said with the conjunction vav, to say that besides the authority granted to him to acquire her through betrothal, for she is acquired through it, he also acquires her to sell her as an amah." "<b>A man, his daughter.</b> They said, of blessed memory (Sotah 23a): ״a man״ — to exclude a woman, that she may not sell her daughter. And the word <b>et</b> may be explained according to what they said, of blessed memory (Mekhilta): the man is permitted to sell himself, but not the woman. And this is what the verse says: <b>and when a man sells</b> — meaning, et himself and also his daughter; and if it had said 'when a man sells his daughter,' nothing would have been heard except the sale of his daughter, and not as mentioned." "<b>As an amah.</b> This is explained according to their statement in tractate Kiddushin (Kiddushin 4a) that even an aylonit daughter — her father has the authority to sell her. And this is what it says <b>as an amah</b>, meaning: even though she is not fit to be married, as long as she is fit to be an amah. Or it may intend, according to what they said, of blessed memory (Tosefta Bekhorot 4), that the tumtum and the androginos are not sold — neither as the sale of a Hebrew slave nor as a Hebrew amah. Therefore it says <b>as an amah</b>, meaning: if she is clearly an amah and not a tumtum or androginos." "<b>She shall not go out as the going out of the slaves.</b> Our Rabbis, of blessed memory (Kiddushin 16a), explained it as the going out of a Canaanite slave through tooth and eye — end. And their reason is that Scripture does not call someone a 'slave' without qualification except a Canaanite, for an Israelite has no name of absolute slavery. And the plain sense of the verse appears to me to be that the intention is <b>she shall not go out as the going out of the slaves</b> who do not go out until they have served six years, and if the father dies they serve before the son; but if the master dies, she goes out to freedom even if she did not stay six." "<b>Or</b> it may intend to say <b>she shall not go out as the going out of the slaves</b> mentioned immediately above, who in the seventh year go out — but there she acquired her place, that the master may designate her (Kiddushin 14b), and she does not go out even after a hundred years except through the master's death or a bill of divorce. And therefore it concluded by saying <b>if she is bad in his eyes, etc.</b> And it is difficult: until the verse mentions her yi'ud, only then is it justified to say ״if she is bad in his eyes, whom he did not designate for her.״ But according to our words it comes out correctly, for when it says <b>she shall not go out</b>, there it hinted at the yi'ud, and upon this it says <b>if she is bad</b>, etc. Also, he was not concerned about error, that one might mistakenly apply to her the rights that HaShem recorded for the slaves — namely, going out in the seventh year and the Jubilee year — for by concluding ״if she is bad״, etc., through this he pointed with a finger that it is for the yi'ud of the husband that he said she acquired her place. And he further added to remove the error by what the verse says (Deuteronomy 15:12): ״your brother the Hebrew, or the Hebrew woman״ — behold, it equated the Hebrew amah to the Hebrew slave, to apply what is stated regarding the Hebrew man to the Hebrew woman." "And this interpretation involves no contradiction to the words of our Sages regarding halakhah — except that in resolving the verse, license is granted to us from them to interpret the verses in whatever manner [suits], so long as it does not contradict the halakhot, for all of them came to them from Sinai. And here the halakhah applies, for the law of tooth and eye applies only to a Canaanite slave and not to an Israelite. And the reason appears to be that a Hebrew slave has the privilege that he should not go free by tooth and eye, for his master is liable to pay him the value of his eye and his tooth and the rest of the things, and the [remaining service] will count for more — and all the more so when this occurs after years have passed of the six years of his service, and sometimes the matter will occur at the very end. Therefore HaShem established that a Hebrew slave shall receive the value of his eye — unlike a Canaanite slave, whose body is fully acquired by the master, who conducts himself toward him with the known leniency corresponding to the aspect of holiness. And these matters are ancient."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:31:41.176107784Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}